        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is Buying a Treadmill Worth It? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Usually yes, if three things are true.</strong> You would otherwise pay for a
            mid-priced gym mainly to use its cardio machines; you will realistically use a home
            treadmill two or more times a week; and you plan to keep doing so for at least two to
            three years. Under those conditions a mid-range machine typically breaks even against
            the membership somewhere between eighteen months and three years, and everything after
            that is close to free cardio.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Usually no, in three other situations.</strong> If your gym costs less than
            about $25 a month, the payback period stretches past the useful life of many machines.
            If you go to the gym for weights, classes or a pool and would keep the membership
            anyway, a treadmill is an additional cost rather than a replacement. And if you are not
            yet in the habit of exercising, a treadmill is a large bet on a habit that does not
            exist yet.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page works through the arithmetic with example figures, then spends
            rather more time on the parts that a spreadsheet misses — how likely you are to keep
            using it, what a gym gives you beyond a belt, what the treadmill costs after the
            purchase, and what you get back if you sell it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-ledger" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Two Sides of the Ledger</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Gym memberships, as typical US ranges.</strong> Budget chains commonly charge
            roughly $10 to $30 a month, often with an annual fee on top. Mid-tier gyms with a fuller
            range of equipment and some classes tend to sit around $40 to $70 a month. Premium
            clubs and boutique studios run from roughly $100 to $250 a month or more. Joining fees
            range from nothing during a promotion to $100 or so, and many contracts add an annual
            "maintenance" or "enhancement" fee of a similar size.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmills, as typical tiers.</strong> Walking pads run roughly $200 to $600,
            budget folding treadmills $400 to $900, mid-range home treadmills $1,000 to $2,000,
            and premium machines $2,000 to $4,000. Our
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cost guide</a>
            explains what each tier buys. For a like-for-like replacement of a gym treadmill —
            something you can run on regularly — the honest comparison is with the mid-range tier,
            not the cheapest machine in a sale.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The asymmetry.</strong> A gym is almost entirely a running cost: you pay every
            month and stop paying when you leave. A treadmill is almost entirely an upfront cost
            with a small running cost and a partial refund at the end when you sell it. That
            difference in shape matters more than the totals, and it is why the decision is
            really about how confident you are in your own future behaviour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="break-even" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Break-Even Arithmetic, Worked Through</h2>
<p>
            The method is simple. Take the full upfront cost of the treadmill, divide it by the
            monthly saving — the gym fee you stop paying, minus what the treadmill costs to run
            each month — and the answer is the number of months to break even. The examples below
            are illustrations with round numbers, not quotes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running cost of a home treadmill, as an example.</strong> Electricity for
            typical home use is a few dollars a month at most, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">electricity guide</a>
            puts 45 minutes of daily jogging at roughly $3. Routine maintenance runs around $15 to
            $40 a year, as the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            details. Call the combined figure about $5 a month, which is generous for most users.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Example 1: mid-range machine against a mid-tier gym.</strong> A $1,500
            treadmill plus a $60 mat is $1,560 upfront. Against a $50-a-month membership, the
            monthly saving is $50 minus $5, or $45. $1,560 divided by $45 is about 35 months — just
            under three years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Example 2: budget machine against the same gym.</strong> A $700 treadmill plus
            a $60 mat is $760. At the same $45 monthly saving, break-even arrives in about 17
            months. The catch is that a budget machine is closer to the end of its life by then,
            particularly under regular running, which our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifespan guide</a>
            covers.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Example 3: mid-range machine against a budget gym.</strong> $1,560 upfront
            against a $25 membership gives a monthly saving of $20, and a break-even of 78 months
            — six and a half years. Against a $15 gym, the saving is $10 a month and break-even is
            thirteen years, which is longer than most home machines are kept. Against a cheap gym,
            a treadmill rarely wins on money alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Example 4: two members of the same household.</strong> Two $50 memberships
            replaced by one $1,560 treadmill saves about $95 a month, and break-even comes in
            around 16 to 17 months. A shared machine is where the arithmetic is strongest, provided
            both people genuinely use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Example 5: adding resale.</strong> Treadmills typically sell for roughly half
            their original price after about two years. If the $1,500 machine in Example 1 is sold
            at that point for around $750, the net cost of two years of use is about $810 plus
            running costs — against $1,200 of membership over the same period. Even a buyer who
            stops after two years can come out ahead, as long as they actually sell it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="will-you-use-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Variable That Decides It: Will You Use It?</h2>
<p>
            Every example above assumes the treadmill is used for as long as the arithmetic needs.
            That is the assumption most worth questioning, because the home treadmill that becomes
            a very expensive clothes rail is a cliché for a reason. Gyms are not immune either —
            plenty of memberships are paid for months after the last visit — but the failure looks
            different.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An unused gym membership is a recurring loss you can stop.</strong> Cancel it
            and the bleeding ends, contract terms permitting. <strong>An unused treadmill is a sunk
            cost you can only partly recover</strong>, by selling it at a discount and arranging
            for a 250 lb object to leave the house. So the treadmill carries more downside if you
            stop, and more upside if you continue.
          </p>
          <p>
            A useful way to think about it is as a probability rather than a plan. If you are
            honestly about 50/50 on still using the machine in a year, Example 1 is not a three-year
            payback with certainty; it is a coin toss between a good deal and an expensive one. The
            people for whom a treadmill reliably pays are those who already exercise consistently
            and want to remove friction from a routine that exists.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The cheap test.</strong> If you are unsure, spend eight weeks doing the routine
            you intend to do at home — on a gym treadmill, on a pay-as-you-go pass, or walking
            outside. If you keep it up for eight weeks with the extra friction of getting there,
            removing that friction is likely to help. If the routine collapses in week three, a
            treadmill in the spare room is unlikely to rescue it, and the money is better kept.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-gym-offers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What a Gym Gives You That a Treadmill Does Not</h2>
<p>
            Most gym memberships are not really treadmill subscriptions, and comparing them as if
            they were flatters the treadmill.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Strength equipment.</strong> Racks, benches, a full set of dumbbells and
            cable machines cost thousands to replicate at home and take a room. For anyone whose
            training includes lifting, this is the main reason to keep a gym.</li>
            <li><strong>Variety of cardio.</strong> Rowers, bikes, ellipticals and stair climbers
            alongside the treadmills. If you like to rotate, one home machine is a narrower
            offering. Our <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a>
            comparison covers the most common alternative.</li>
            <li><strong>Classes and coaching.</strong> Structure, instruction and other people
            expecting you to turn up — a genuine motivational asset for many.</li>
            <li><strong>Facilities.</strong> Pools, saunas, showers, courts and childcare, where
            offered.</li>
            <li><strong>Separation.</strong> A place you go specifically to exercise. Some people
            train better away from the distractions of home, and that is a real effect rather than
            an excuse.</li>
            <li><strong>Commercial machines.</strong> Gym treadmills are usually heavier, more
            stable and better cushioned than most home machines, and someone else maintains them.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical consequence: if you would keep a gym membership for any of these
            reasons, the treadmill does not replace it, and the break-even arithmetic above does
            not apply. The only exception is if the treadmill lets you <em>downgrade</em> — say,
            from a $60 full-service gym to a $20 budget gym used purely for weights. Then the
            monthly saving is the $40 difference, and the sums work much like Example 1.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="convenience" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Convenience Is the Treadmill’s Real Advantage</h2>
<p>
            The strongest argument for a home treadmill is not the money. It is time, and the way
            removing small obstacles changes how often people actually exercise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Time, as an example.</strong> A gym fifteen minutes away costs half an hour of
            travel per session before you have changed or waited for a machine. At three sessions
            a week that is about 78 hours a year — roughly two working weeks — spent getting there
            and back. A treadmill in the next room turns that into a thirty-second walk.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Availability.</strong> A home machine is free at 5am, at 11pm, in a storm, in
            the dark, with a sleeping baby upstairs or a child doing homework at the kitchen
            table. For parents, shift workers and anyone whose free time arrives in unpredictable
            twenty-minute gaps, this is often decisive. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-running-outside/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs running outside</a>
            guide covers the same advantage against outdoor running.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No queue, no audience.</strong> January waits for a treadmill are a real thing,
            and some people simply prefer to walk slowly at incline or work through a beginner
            programme without being watched.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Combining with other things.</strong> A home treadmill can sit in front of a
            television or under a standing desk, which turns time you were already spending into
            walking time. That is not a trick available at a gym.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Hidden Costs on Both Sides</h2>
<p>
            Neither option costs only its headline price. These are the additions to put into the
            sums, as typical figures.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmill: delivery and assembly.</strong> Curbside delivery is often included,
            but room-of-choice delivery and assembly can add roughly $150 to $300.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmill: maintenance and repairs.</strong> Routine upkeep is cheap; the
            expensive failures — motor and controller board — mostly arrive on machines that were
            never lubricated, and can cost several hundred dollars outside warranty. Budget a belt
            and deck replacement somewhere around year seven on a machine you keep. The
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost breakdown</a>
            gives the full picture.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmill: subscriptions.</strong> The cost that wrecks the comparison most
            often. Many touchscreen machines are built around a monthly app membership, and a $40
            subscription is $480 a year — at which point you are paying for a mid-tier gym again,
            plus the machine. Check what the treadmill does without it, read our assessment of
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFit is worth it</a>,
            or start from machines that work fully
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">without a subscription</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmill: space.</strong> A full-size machine with proper clearance needs a
            floor area of roughly 12 by 6 feet, as our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            works through. If that space has another use, or you would otherwise rent a smaller
            home, it has a cost even if nobody bills you for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Gym: fees beyond the monthly rate.</strong> Joining fees, annual fees, charges
            for classes or personal training, and cancellation terms that can require notice or a
            final payment. Contracts with a minimum term turn a monthly cost into a commitment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Gym: getting there.</strong> Fuel, parking or transit fares, multiplied by every
            visit. Small per trip, rarely small across a year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resale" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Resale Value and the Exit Cost</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill is one of the few household purchases that reliably sells secondhand, and
            the resale value changes the arithmetic more than people expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a typical pattern, a well-kept machine sells for roughly half its original price
            after a couple of years and about a third after three to five, after which the price
            flattens because what remains is largely the frame and motor. Recognisable mid-range
            and premium models hold value better than budget machines, which have consumed more of
            a shorter life by the time they are sold. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used guide</a>
            looks at the same curve from the buyer’s side.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two practical caveats. First, selling takes effort: photographs, a listing, viewings,
            and a buyer who has to arrange to move 200 to 300 lbs. Many sellers accept a lower
            price to avoid the hassle. Second, a machine that was never maintained sells for less
            and sells more slowly, because informed buyers ask about lubrication and listen to the
            motor. If the machine is at the end of its life, disposal rather than sale may be the
            exit, and our
            <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">disposal guide</a>
            covers the options.
          </p>
          <p>
            The gym’s exit cost is different: nothing to sell, but potentially a notice period or
            a cancellation fee. Read the contract before signing rather than when you want to
            leave.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="household" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Households, Space and Noise</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Several users multiply the saving.</strong> One machine shared by two or three
            regular users replaces two or three memberships, and the break-even shortens
            proportionally. If more than one person will run on it, buy for the heaviest and
            tallest user and for the combined weekly hours — a busier machine needs a sturdier
            motor and frame, which pushes the purchase toward the mid-range tier.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Apartments and upstairs rooms.</strong> Footfall noise travels through floors
            more than motor noise travels through air. A neighbour below can turn a sound financial
            decision into an unworkable one, so consider when you will use it and what is
            underneath. Our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills guide</a>
            covers what helps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walkers versus runners.</strong> If you mostly walk, the treadmill side of the
            ledger gets cheaper: a smaller, quieter machine or a walking pad does the job, and the
            break-even shortens. If you run hard, a cheap machine is a false economy and the
            mid-range figures above are the realistic ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy, and Who Should Keep the Gym</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a treadmill if</strong> you already exercise consistently and most of it is
            walking or running; your gym costs $40 a month or more and you use little else there;
            two or more people in the house will use it; your schedule makes travel to a gym the
            main obstacle; or you can downgrade to a budget gym for weights and pocket the
            difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the gym if</strong> strength training, classes or a pool are a large part
            of why you go; your membership is already cheap; you train better away from home; you
            do not have the space for a full-size machine with clearance; or you are not yet in a
            regular routine and want something you can cancel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do both if</strong> the treadmill handles the frequent, short sessions on
            weekday mornings and a cheap gym handles two strength sessions a week. For many people
            this is the best arrangement, and the arithmetic works if the treadmill allows the move
            from a mid-tier gym to a budget one.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you do buy, our
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to how much to spend</a>
            helps match the tier to how you will use it — which is the other way a treadmill
            purchase goes wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cheaper-routes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Cheaper Ways to Test the Decision</h2>
<p>
            The break-even sums assume a new machine at full retail price. There are ways to lower
            the stake and shorten the payback.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy used.</strong> A two-to-four-year-old mid-range machine often costs about
            half of new, which halves the break-even period. The risk is entirely in the inspection,
            and our new vs used guide covers how to manage it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy in a sale.</strong> Fitness equipment is often discounted after the
            new-year rush and around major retail events. Our
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">timing guide</a>
            goes through the calendar.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start with a walking pad</strong> if your goal is daily walking rather than
            running. A few hundred dollars tests the habit, and the pad still has a use under a
            desk if you later buy a full treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the right machine once.</strong> A $500 machine bought to save money and
            replaced at year two costs more than a $1,200 machine kept for eight. The cheapest
            option at the till is not the cheapest over the comparison period.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>On money alone, a home treadmill beats a mid-priced gym membership</strong>
            within roughly one and a half to three years for a single regular user, faster for a
            household, and faster still if you sell it afterwards. Against a budget gym under
            about $25 a month, it rarely pays back on money alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On usefulness, it depends on what you go to the gym for.</strong> If the answer
            is the treadmill, buy one. If the answer is weights, classes or the pool, a treadmill
            is an addition rather than a replacement — worth it for convenience, not for savings.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On risk, prove the habit first.</strong> Eight weeks of the routine you intend
            to follow, done with the inconvenience of getting to it, tells you more about whether a
            treadmill will pay for itself than any spreadsheet. If the habit survives that, buy a
            mid-range machine without a mandatory subscription, maintain it, and enjoy what is
            effectively free cardio from year three onward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is buying a treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes if you will use it at least twice a week for two to three years and the treadmill is the main thing you would otherwise pay a gym for. In that case a mid-range machine typically breaks even against a mid-priced membership within about one and a half to three years. If you are not yet exercising regularly, or you go to the gym mainly for weights or classes, it usually is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill cheaper than a gym membership?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over time, usually, against a mid-priced gym. As an example, a $1,500 treadmill plus a mat against a $50 monthly membership breaks even in just under three years once running costs of around $5 a month are allowed for. Against a budget gym at $15 to $25 a month, the payback stretches to six years or more, which is longer than many people keep a machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take for a treadmill to pay for itself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the full upfront cost — machine, mat, delivery — by the monthly gym fee you stop paying minus roughly $5 a month in electricity and maintenance. Using example figures, that gives around 17 months for a $700 machine and 35 months for a $1,500 machine against a $50 gym, and about half that if two people share the machine and both cancel memberships.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a treadmill or join a gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a treadmill if you already exercise consistently, mostly walk or run, and travel time is your main obstacle. Join or keep a gym if you want strength equipment, classes or a pool, have limited space at home, or are still building the habit and want something you can cancel. Many people do best with a home treadmill plus a cheap gym for weights.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do treadmills hold their resale value?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reasonably well for a household appliance. As a typical pattern, a well-kept machine sells for roughly half its original price after about two years and around a third after three to five, then levels off. Recognisable mid-range and premium models sell more easily than budget ones, and a machine with a maintenance history sells faster than one without.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the hidden costs of owning a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Delivery and assembly, often $150 to $300 if not included; a mat; routine maintenance of roughly $15 to $40 a year; electricity of a few dollars a month; occasional repairs outside warranty; and, on many touchscreen machines, a monthly app subscription that can cost as much as a gym membership. The space it occupies is a cost too.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Comparing a Treadmill With a Gym</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most bad decisions here come from comparing the wrong things, not from bad arithmetic.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing against a gym you would keep anyway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If you go to the gym for weights, classes or the pool, cancelling it is not on the table and the treadmill saves nothing. The only fair comparison in that case is against a cheaper gym you could downgrade to, and the monthly saving is the difference between the two memberships.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the subscription</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill bought for its touchscreen often needs a monthly app membership to be useful, and at around $40 a month that is the price of a mid-tier gym. Add it into the break-even sum, or choose a machine that works fully without one.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying to create a habit rather than to support one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill removes friction from a routine that exists; it rarely creates one. Test the routine for eight weeks first, at a gym or outdoors. If it survives that, the treadmill will very likely pay for itself.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the cheapest machine to shorten the payback</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A budget machine breaks even sooner on paper but may wear out under regular running before it has paid for itself. A mid-range machine kept for eight years almost always costs less per session than a cheap one replaced at year two.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

